	abstract = {Within archimedean ℓ-groups, “G ∈ SW∗” means there are H with strong unit (H ∈ W∗) and an embedding G ≤ H. A. Theorem (6.1). For X a Tychonoff space (or completely regular locale), C(X) ∈ SW∗ iff X is pseudocompact (which means C(X) ∈ W∗). But any G ∈ SW∗ embeds into various C(X), and any C(X) contains many H ∈ W∗. We define cardinal invariants \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}G\$, \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}G\$, λG which generalize respectively, the bounding and dominating numbers for \${\textbackslash}mathbb \{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}mathbb \{N\}\}\$, \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$and \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}\$, and the π-weight of a topological space. B. Theorem (6.3, 7.5). SupposeG ≤ C(X), and X contains densely \${\textbackslash}bigcup \_\{I\} X\_\{i\}\$, the Xi compact. Then G ∈ SW∗ if either ({\textbar}I{\textbar} = ω and \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}G {\textless} {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$) or (\${\textbar}I{\textbar} {\textless} {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$and \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}G = {\textbackslash}omega \$). This “\${\textbackslash}omega , {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$symmetry” fades a bit in the following, where \${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{p\}\$is the pseudo-intersection number for \${\textbackslash}mathbb \{R\}{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}mathbb \{N\}\}\$(\${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{p\} {\textbackslash}le {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$, with = under Martin’s Axiom). C. Theorem (8.2). G ∈ SW∗ if either \$({\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}G = {\textbackslash}omega \$and \${\textbackslash}lambda G {\textless} {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{b\}\$) or (\${\textbackslash}mathfrak \{d\}G {\textless} {\textbackslash}mathfrak \{p\}\$andλG = ω). Examples (§9) show limits on the hypotheses in B and C.},
	
