	abstract = {In a locally \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$-presentable category, with \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$ a regular cardinal, classes of objects that are injective with respect to a family of morphisms whose domains and codomains are \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$-presentable, are known to be characterized by their closure under products, \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$-directed colimits and \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$-pure subobjects. Replacing the strict commutativity of diagrams by "commutativity up to \${\textbackslash}textbackslashvarepsilon\$", this paper provides an "approximate version" of this characterization for categories enriched over metric spaces. It entails a detailed discussion of the needed \${\textbackslash}textbackslashvarepsilon\$-generalizations of the notion of \${\textbackslash}textbackslashlambda\$-purity. The categorical theory is being applied to the locally \${\textbackslash}textbackslashaleph\_1\$-presentable category of Banach spaces and their linear operators of norm at most 1, culminating in a largely categorical proof for the existence of the so-called Gurarii Banach space.},
